00:17:28.359 --> 00:17:32.530
Yes, but-- but, we don't have
to worry about the hardware

00:17:32.530 --> 00:17:33.540
components and things.

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:33.910
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: That's good.

00:17:33.910 --> 00:17:34.270
That's good.

00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:36.686
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: We can just
focus on clicking a button,

00:17:36.686 --> 00:17:37.570
getting capacity.

00:17:37.570 --> 00:17:40.450
And I don't have to
worry about that guy

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:43.401
that we hired to deal with
all of the hardware-specific

00:17:43.401 --> 00:17:43.900
things.

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:45.608
We've retasked him,
and now he's actually

00:17:45.608 --> 00:17:46.840
working in our devops group.

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:47.750
We merged the teams.

00:17:47.750 --> 00:17:47.920
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: Nice.

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:49.240
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND:
How do you like that?

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:50.140
Pretty cool, huh?

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:50.950
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: I'm
still concerned about--

00:17:50.950 --> 00:17:53.500
because you mentioned
cloud-native 10 years ago.

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:54.677
And I am not sure--

00:17:54.677 --> 00:17:55.510
I keep hearing that.

00:17:55.510 --> 00:17:57.551
I keep hearing people are
producing in the cloud,

00:17:57.551 --> 00:18:01.130
but like in an elastic
way, in a on-demand way.

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:02.702
So now I'm concerned.

00:18:02.702 --> 00:18:04.410
Are we getting the
most out of the cloud?

00:18:04.410 --> 00:18:06.790
Are we using the cloud
in the best way possible,

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:09.329
engineering team?

00:18:09.329 --> 00:18:10.870
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND:
Well to be honest,

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:14.360
probably not necessarily.

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:16.360
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: So what
else can we look into?

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:18.484
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: Let's
go back to what I actually

00:18:18.484 --> 00:18:20.890
need the cloud to do.

00:18:20.890 --> 00:18:24.100
So what does this cloud
thing actually provide,

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:25.970
and what do I need
it to actually do?

00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:29.060
How do I get the most value
out of this investment?

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:33.040
Well, what I need, what we
need at the wonderful Vandelay

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:37.180
Industries is reliability,
scalability, automation,

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:38.290
standardization.

00:18:38.290 --> 00:18:41.020
I need the thing to stay
up when I put it up.

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:43.600
I need the thing to scale
so that it doesn't go down

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:45.380
and, thus, does not stay up.

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:49.330
I also need to automate it
because we don't-- we only have

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:52.480
a handful of people, and we've
got a lot of web pages now.

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:55.610
So I want to push a button
and have something work.

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:58.090
Standardization
though-- I absolutely

00:18:58.090 --> 00:18:59.470
need standardization.

00:18:59.470 --> 00:19:01.570
The reason I need
standardization is we

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:05.890
learned one framework,
one infrastructure thing

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:06.910
and then it changed.

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:08.300
We went to another thing.

00:19:08.300 --> 00:19:09.557
We had to redo everything.

00:19:09.557 --> 00:19:11.140
I don't want to have
to do that again.

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:14.150
I want to just push a button
and then have it work.

00:19:14.150 --> 00:19:16.057
As a matter of
fact, I want no ops.

00:19:16.057 --> 00:19:17.140
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: Do what?

00:19:17.140 --> 00:19:17.740
I'm sorry, what?

00:19:17.740 --> 00:19:18.700
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND:
No operations.

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:19.741
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: Ah, OK.

00:19:19.741 --> 00:19:20.530
I like that.

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:21.610
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: How
amazing would that be?

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:24.006
I mean, imagine a world
where I could push a button

00:19:24.006 --> 00:19:25.630
and the computers
would run themselves.

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:26.840
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: I like it.

00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:29.320
So you guys would be
focusing on, instead of

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:31.200
operations and hardware and--

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:31.880
I like it.

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:32.380
I like it.

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:33.220
We should do that.

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:35.360
So do you think we should
look at that modern IT

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:36.924
stack with today's tools maybe?

00:19:36.924 --> 00:19:38.590
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND:
Yeah, all right, so I

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:40.660
had a bit of a think.

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:43.349
And I think I've come up with
some interesting, interesting

00:19:43.349 --> 00:19:44.640
components I think you'll like.

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:48.250
FERNANDO SANCHEZ:
OK, let's look at it.

00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:50.950
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: This is what
I think a present a modern IT

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:53.030
stack is going to look like.

00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:55.710
Let's leverage this
thing called Kubernetes.

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:58.570
Let's take advantage of
better virtual networks,

00:19:58.570 --> 00:20:00.030
better virtual storage.

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:02.470
Lets leverage things
like managed security,

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:05.385
hosted monitoring and logging,
and a boatload of automation.

00:20:05.385 --> 00:20:06.760
Because here's
the thing, I don't

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:10.180
want to have to power an entire
subsection of infrastructure

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:12.790
just to manage the
rest of infrastructure

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:13.720
that I need to deploy.

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:17.587
I just want it to work for
me, which is pretty darn cool,

00:20:17.587 --> 00:20:19.420
especially when you
consider that Kubernetes

00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:21.340
does a lot of the work for us.

00:20:21.340 --> 00:20:23.140
It's kind of an
interesting concept.

00:20:23.140 --> 00:20:25.090
We just have to containerize
some applications

00:20:25.090 --> 00:20:27.280
in here and there, and I
think we should be good.

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:28.880
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: All
right, so we have an idea.

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:30.088
I think we should work on it.

00:20:30.088 --> 00:20:33.220
We should probably get our LAMP
stack and use all these things

00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:34.150
and see how it looks.

00:20:34.150 --> 00:20:34.970
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: We should

00:20:34.970 --> 00:20:36.720
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: OK,
so let's work on it.

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:38.080
Let's work on it--

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:39.950
our LAMP solution.

00:20:39.950 --> 00:20:41.950
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: So
now, my colleague Fernando

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:45.520
is going to stop being the CTO
of this wonderful prestigious

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:47.320
Vandelay Industries
and is instead

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:50.800
going to resume his role
as a Google customer

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:52.517
engineer alongside myself.

00:20:52.517 --> 00:20:54.850
And we're going to talk about
the actual linked solution

00:20:54.850 --> 00:20:57.160
that we came up for,
for any industry that

00:20:57.160 --> 00:20:59.650
wants to take advantage of all
of the things we mentioned,

00:20:59.650 --> 00:21:02.890
all of the needs that we want,
and get all of the benefits

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:04.510
that we talked about as well.

00:21:04.510 --> 00:21:05.170
FERNANDO SANCHEZ:
OK, so we wanted

00:21:05.170 --> 00:21:06.700
to get our LAMP applications.

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:08.380
So that should stay
the same, because we

00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:10.810
have a lot of stuff developed
for Vandelay Industries that

00:21:10.810 --> 00:21:12.850
has, whether we've been
working in Drupal or WordPress,

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:13.840
we still want to do that.

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:15.410
But we want to put
that in containers.

00:21:15.410 --> 00:21:16.620
We want to put
that in containers

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:18.510
so that it's immutable, so that
we don't have to change it.

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:19.760
We don't have to configure it.

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:20.425
We keep it.

00:21:20.425 --> 00:21:20.770
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: Yeah.

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:22.330
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: But we don't
want to handle containers.

00:21:22.330 --> 00:21:24.400
We don't want to go from
managing VMs to managing

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:24.900
containers.

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:27.250
So as much as we can
use a managed container

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:30.190
service, somebody that handles
the cluster for us, well

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:31.465
that would be great.

00:21:31.465 --> 00:21:33.340
The other key piece for
LAMP is the database.

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:34.964
That's where the
information is, right?

00:21:34.964 --> 00:21:37.480
That's the core of the
system, the spinal tap.

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:39.620
If we can remove the
operations piece,

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:42.040
if we can get some good
sleep without having

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:44.736
to work in backups,
in keeping it up,

00:21:44.736 --> 00:21:47.360
in high availability and things
like that, that would be great.

00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:50.710
So let's look at a
managed version of MySQL.

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:53.290
The other thing that
LAMP has is storage.

00:21:53.290 --> 00:21:54.710
And storage has two functions.

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:56.595
The first one is where
LAMP keeps state.

00:21:56.595 --> 00:21:57.970
That's where your
virtual machine

00:21:57.970 --> 00:21:59.392
would save the configuration.

00:21:59.392 --> 00:22:00.850
And that's where
it also would save

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:02.590
some static content,
or your images,

00:22:02.590 --> 00:22:04.720
or your videos, or anything.

00:22:04.720 --> 00:22:07.060
So how about we get a cloud
storage solution and we

00:22:07.060 --> 00:22:10.840
plug the solution into
the rest of the cluster?

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:13.120
And the last piece that
we wanted is automation.

00:22:13.120 --> 00:22:14.710
How can we put all
this together so

00:22:14.710 --> 00:22:16.760
that we can create clusters
with a single click

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:18.760
and have all the configuration
for a [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:20.946
code in a repo somewhere
so that if somebody wants

00:22:20.946 --> 00:22:22.820
to make changes to the
cluster, you shouldn't

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:24.240
have to touch the cluster.

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:26.250
You touch the code,
and then redeploy.

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:26.890
OK?

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:28.450
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: But
the most important thing

00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:31.120
of all of these things on here,
the thing that makes or breaks

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:33.820
this solution, is Kubernetes.

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:36.100
I mean, like a lot of
the other stuff, sure,

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:38.665
but this is like the
absolute crucial component

00:22:38.665 --> 00:22:39.387
that has to work.

00:22:39.387 --> 00:22:40.470
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: I agree.

00:22:40.470 --> 00:22:43.190
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: I think
it's a good solution as well.

00:22:43.190 --> 00:22:44.200
FERNANDO SANCHEZ:
So why Kubernetes?

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:44.930
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND:
OK, Kubernetes

00:22:44.930 --> 00:22:46.250
is kind of interesting.

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:47.980
First of all, it's a standard.

00:22:47.980 --> 00:22:51.000
So if we write a
manifest to Kubernetes,

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:54.210
it will work on any Kubernetes
offering that's out there.

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:57.580
It will work on any Kubernetes
environment that somebody has,

00:22:57.580 --> 00:23:00.200
provided the environment
works and whatnot.

00:23:00.200 --> 00:23:03.220
Also, it maintains our desired
state through failures,

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:04.660
meaning I don't
have to get paged.

00:23:04.660 --> 00:23:07.180
Kubernetes will take care
of restarting things.

00:23:07.180 --> 00:23:10.000
So that reliability
component is just

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:13.120
taken care of by the automation
that Kubernetes ends up

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:14.130
providing.

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:15.400
Also it provide scalability.

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:17.030
They kind of go hand-in-hand.

00:23:17.030 --> 00:23:20.630
So if we're getting a
massive influx of traffic,

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:23.710
we can take advantage of
Kubernetes scaling capabilities

00:23:23.710 --> 00:23:25.010
and autoscaling capabilities--

00:23:25.010 --> 00:23:26.260
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: I like that.

00:23:26.260 --> 00:23:28.385
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: --I know
Vandelay Industries was

00:23:28.385 --> 00:23:30.690
a super-duper interested
in that in the past--

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:34.730
to spin up more infrastructure
and consume it as needed.

00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:37.180
Also, it's relatively
easy to program.

00:23:37.180 --> 00:23:39.190
I say relatively
because, yeah, it

00:23:39.190 --> 00:23:41.440
has a quirky sort of
YAML syntax and whatnot

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:43.190
that you have to
fiddle around with.

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:45.280
But once you get it
done, all of your states,

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:47.710
even really complex
applications,

00:23:47.710 --> 00:23:50.620
can actually be mapped out
inside of Kubernetes services.

00:23:50.620 --> 00:23:52.480
There are some cons though.

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:53.744
It's hard to manage.

00:23:53.744 --> 00:23:55.160
And, in fact, I'll
stand over here

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:56.650
so people can actually see.

00:23:56.650 --> 00:23:58.000
It's hard to manage.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:01.190
It's a big distributed system
with lots of moving parts.

00:24:01.190 --> 00:24:03.940
It's difficult to
upgrade because it's

00:24:03.940 --> 00:24:06.340
a large distributed system
with many moving parts.

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:09.890
It's very difficult to
scale for the same thing.

00:24:09.890 --> 00:24:11.980
It also requires other
infrastructure services

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:16.300
to function, meaning that you
need a VM to put Kubernetes in.

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:19.480
It needs a computer to
actually run the code that

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:21.490
powers the rest of the cluster.

00:24:21.490 --> 00:24:25.707
Also it prefers stateless
applications, not applications

00:24:25.707 --> 00:24:27.790
that have state that need
to grab a bunch of stuff

00:24:27.790 --> 00:24:28.990
on the backend.

00:24:28.990 --> 00:24:32.170
But I think I can solve
most of these issues.

00:24:34.870 --> 00:24:37.260
Let's take a look at
Google Kubernetes engine,

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:39.300
otherwise known as GKE.

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:41.844
What's really great is we
get all of the same pros.

00:24:41.844 --> 00:24:42.760
It's still a standard.

00:24:42.760 --> 00:24:44.730
It still maintains
your desired state.

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:47.340
It still provides
scalability and reliability.

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:48.900
You get a bonus
that it's managed

00:24:48.900 --> 00:24:51.090
by Google's legendary SRE team.

00:24:51.090 --> 00:24:55.360
You can actually go over
there and take a look at them.

00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:58.589
But it still prefers
stateless applications.

00:24:58.589 --> 00:24:59.880
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: I understand.

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:01.950
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: So this
solves most of the issues

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:04.170
that we have in terms of
managing our infrastructure,

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:05.880
except for that stateless part.

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:07.470
Do you have any
ideas about that?

00:25:07.470 --> 00:25:07.890
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: I do.

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:09.306
The thing with
legacy applications

00:25:09.306 --> 00:25:11.250
is they were not
written to be stateless.

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:14.460
They were written to save state
to some sort of file system.

00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:17.482
You have comf files, and
you have state files.

00:25:17.482 --> 00:25:19.190
So if instead of
running a single server,

00:25:19.190 --> 00:25:22.495
we want to run 100, 1,000 of
containers that supposedly

00:25:22.495 --> 00:25:23.870
run in the same
application, they

00:25:23.870 --> 00:25:25.740
should be sharing the state.

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:27.090
And containers are stateless.

00:25:27.090 --> 00:25:29.780
If you reboot a container, the
file system in the container

00:25:29.780 --> 00:25:30.300
is gone.

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:31.980
That would mean that if we
put up a Drupal container,

00:25:31.980 --> 00:25:34.355
and we configure it, and we
start putting stuff in there,

00:25:34.355 --> 00:25:35.710
and we reboot it, it's gone.

00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:38.512
So how do we simulate, how
do we get legacy software

00:25:38.512 --> 00:25:40.470
and make it believe that
it's running in legacy

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:43.800
infrastructure, while still
we provide the advantages

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:44.904
of modern architectures?

00:25:44.904 --> 00:25:46.320
So what we need
to do is basically

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:49.560
get a shared file system and
make all those containers

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:51.990
access that shared
file system and make

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:54.990
it available for all of them
to read at the same time

00:25:54.990 --> 00:25:57.382
and for all of them to
write at the same time.

00:25:57.382 --> 00:25:58.840
So how do we do
that in Kubernetes?

00:25:58.840 --> 00:26:00.780
Well, that's basically
a file system

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:02.750
mount that it's read
many, write many.

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:04.560
Is everyone familiar with that?

00:26:04.560 --> 00:26:06.356
So there are a
number of solutions

00:26:06.356 --> 00:26:07.480
that allows you to do that.

00:26:07.480 --> 00:26:09.780
One of them, for example,
is as simple as NFS.

00:26:09.780 --> 00:26:11.330
An NFS could be an NFS server.

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:13.350
But NFS could be
any storage solution

00:26:13.350 --> 00:26:14.910
that supports an NFS interface.

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:17.610
I'm sure that you guys have your
favorite storage vendors that

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:21.300
can have a huge system,
provide a super secure NFS

00:26:21.300 --> 00:26:23.050
and you can connect
it to a cluster.

00:26:23.050 --> 00:26:25.320
Or you can just use the
on-demand NFS server

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:26.750
by Google Cloud File Server.

00:26:26.750 --> 00:26:30.720
We provide you with storage
managed by the legendary Google

00:26:30.720 --> 00:26:32.100
SRE--

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:34.590
sorry-- team.

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:36.750
So basically, the other
thing that happens

00:26:36.750 --> 00:26:41.331
is that state, it's
for static content

00:26:41.331 --> 00:26:43.830
and it's for configuration, but
the way that many people are

00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:45.740
using LAMP these days,
including Drupal,

00:26:45.740 --> 00:26:47.940
is what is called
a headless CMS.

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:50.460
What that means is Vandelay
Industries, at some point,

00:26:50.460 --> 00:26:52.560
may not like the way
that Drupal and WordPress

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:54.189
look for the frontend.

00:26:54.189 --> 00:26:56.730
They may just want to use Drupal
because it's a great content

00:26:56.730 --> 00:26:57.970
management system.

00:26:57.970 --> 00:27:00.090
It's a system that they
can use for the users

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:01.870
to create content,
to edit content.

00:27:01.870 --> 00:27:06.130
And maybe instead of
offering the UI for Drupal,

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:08.107
the frontend for
Drupal and WordPress,

00:27:08.107 --> 00:27:10.440
maybe they want to use something
like the API for Drupal

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:12.840
and WordPress, put an API
gateway in the middle,

00:27:12.840 --> 00:27:15.030
and then use their
favorite technology

00:27:15.030 --> 00:27:16.830
to write the
frontend, whether it's

00:27:16.830 --> 00:27:18.820
React Redux,
whether its Angular,

00:27:18.820 --> 00:27:20.670
whether it's anything
they want to use.

00:27:20.670 --> 00:27:22.634
So you get the best
of both worlds.

00:27:22.634 --> 00:27:23.550
You get your frontend.

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:25.600
Your frontend calls
the API gateway.

00:27:25.600 --> 00:27:28.380
The API gateway gets the
content from Drupal and out.

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:30.660
What that means is
that storage system

00:27:30.660 --> 00:27:33.180
that has this state
has actually way less

00:27:33.180 --> 00:27:35.060
stringent requirements, right?

00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:37.620
That's not a key
component from your system

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:40.830
because the static content is
probably hosted somewhere else.

00:27:40.830 --> 00:27:43.320
Your React Redux it's probably
hitting the API gateway.

00:27:43.320 --> 00:27:45.760
The API gateway is getting
it from somewhere else.

00:27:45.760 --> 00:27:48.420
So what we're going to get in
that distributed storage system

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:51.254
is just a basic state,
basic configuration.

00:27:51.254 --> 00:27:52.920
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND:
And hold on a second.

00:27:52.920 --> 00:27:54.860
So you're a Google
customer engineer.

00:27:54.860 --> 00:27:56.420
You work in the retail space.

00:27:56.420 --> 00:27:58.715
Do you have actual
real-world customers

00:27:58.715 --> 00:28:00.840
that want to take advantage
of something like this?

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:03.110
It's not just
Vandelay Industries,

00:28:03.110 --> 00:28:04.890
but you actually have
real-world customers

00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:05.980
that would be interested
in this kind of thing?

00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:08.910
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: Yeah, we do
import, export, architecture.

00:28:08.910 --> 00:28:11.430
We actually have a customer
who has three works too,

00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:13.070
that they may be using this.

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:15.460
Unfortunately, they couldn't
make it to the call today.

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:19.060
But this is being live
today, the headless CMS idea.

00:28:19.060 --> 00:28:24.554
Drupal is getting a second life
by being used as headless CMS

00:28:24.554 --> 00:28:26.220
so that you get the
best of both worlds.

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:28.095
The frontend, you can
load your own frontend,

00:28:28.095 --> 00:28:32.040
use an API gateway, and connect
it to Drupal as the CMS.

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:35.387
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: So what's
all of this look like then?

00:28:35.387 --> 00:28:37.470
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: In detail,
what do we have here?

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:40.050
What we have is many containers
running our software.

00:28:40.050 --> 00:28:41.630
So software is
the old processes,

00:28:41.630 --> 00:28:45.780
the content that we developed
for it along the years.

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:47.710
I have the Apache
and the PHP pieces.

00:28:47.710 --> 00:28:50.400
We're running in containers,
and containers running on GKE.

00:28:50.400 --> 00:28:53.730
That means that autoscale on
the map, the horizontal pod

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:56.340
autoscaler autoscales the pods.

00:28:56.340 --> 00:28:58.590
And then the cluster autoscaler
autoscales the nodes.

00:28:58.590 --> 00:29:00.300
If we get a peak in
traffic, the cluster

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:02.870
scales up and down as we want.

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:07.260
The static content is created
with basically a file share.

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:10.410
And that file share is mounted
into Kubernetes with a read

00:29:10.410 --> 00:29:13.950
many, write many mount.

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:17.430
And then on the left side,
or the left side for you,

00:29:17.430 --> 00:29:19.350
we have basically a
managed MySQL database.

00:29:19.350 --> 00:29:21.600
What that means is using
in the cloud SQL service,

00:29:21.600 --> 00:29:23.340
we just hit a
button, get a managed

00:29:23.340 --> 00:29:25.110
database handled by Google.

00:29:25.110 --> 00:29:26.610
We just need to
program the backups.

00:29:26.610 --> 00:29:30.030
We just need to basically
let go and that's

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:31.786
handled by somebody else.

00:29:31.786 --> 00:29:32.910
And we pay for what we use.

00:29:32.910 --> 00:29:33.970
It autoscales.

00:29:33.970 --> 00:29:37.495
We just literally pay
for whatever we're using.

00:29:37.495 --> 00:29:38.870
And then in the
bottom piece, one

00:29:38.870 --> 00:29:40.286
of the things that
we wanted to do

00:29:40.286 --> 00:29:42.400
is not having to
basically clip through UIs

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:46.065
and send many commands and take
weeks to deploy this thing.

00:29:46.065 --> 00:29:47.690
We wanted to automate
this whole thing.

00:29:47.690 --> 00:29:49.430
And in this case, we
used Terraform for that.

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:51.050
Do you want to discuss,
for example, why

00:29:51.050 --> 00:29:52.174
we used Terraform for that.

00:29:52.174 --> 00:29:54.350
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: Well, I
mean, as that devops guy,

00:29:54.350 --> 00:29:56.810
I like using whatever
is available out there

00:29:56.810 --> 00:29:58.580
and whatever the
specific customer might

00:29:58.580 --> 00:29:59.900
have familiarity with.

00:29:59.900 --> 00:30:01.580
So, for example, you
can bring anything

00:30:01.580 --> 00:30:03.330
that you want that
provides infrastructure

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:05.060
as code functionality.

00:30:05.060 --> 00:30:08.000
In the industry, Terraform
tends to be pretty darn popular.

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:10.790
So as a result, we decided
to write our solution

00:30:10.790 --> 00:30:12.740
using Terraform and a
couple of shell scripts

00:30:12.740 --> 00:30:16.010
that actually bootstrap
the entire environment.

00:30:16.010 --> 00:30:18.770
Terraform can be a
little bit difficult

00:30:18.770 --> 00:30:21.740
to get stood up in
the sense that you

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:23.210
have to configure the provider.

00:30:23.210 --> 00:30:24.680
You also have to configure
different backends

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:26.750
and so on and so forth
with different clouds.

00:30:26.750 --> 00:30:29.000
However, once we get all
of these things together,

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:30.916
really you could use
pretty much anything that

00:30:30.916 --> 00:30:32.090
does infrastructure as code.

00:30:32.090 --> 00:30:34.173
Google works a little bit
differently in the sense

00:30:34.173 --> 00:30:36.140
that we'd like partnering
with, and we also

00:30:36.140 --> 00:30:38.090
like supporting a lot
of different open source

00:30:38.090 --> 00:30:38.480
technologies.

00:30:38.480 --> 00:30:39.200
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: Absolutely.

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:40.280
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: So
we want to make sure

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:42.950
that whatever you're already
using in your industry,

00:30:42.950 --> 00:30:45.590
we support and it runs
really well on our platform.

00:30:45.590 --> 00:30:47.780
But if you want
guaranteed compatibility,

00:30:47.780 --> 00:30:49.940
and you want an SLO
associated with things,

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:51.410
or an SLA associated
with things,

00:30:51.410 --> 00:30:53.660
you can use something called
Deployment Manager, which

00:30:53.660 --> 00:30:57.490
is our version of
infrastructure as code.

00:30:57.490 --> 00:30:58.990
FERNANDO SANCHEZ:
OK, and that's it.

00:30:58.990 --> 00:31:00.780
And what we have also is a demo.

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:04.130
And for the demo, we would
like to ask you for a favor.

00:31:04.130 --> 00:31:05.990
If you guys go to
cloudlamp.org--

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:07.220
we decided to do two things.

00:31:07.220 --> 00:31:08.780
The first thing is
get all the code

00:31:08.780 --> 00:31:11.077
that we did for this
and open source it.

00:31:11.077 --> 00:31:13.160
So we basically put it all
in the web for you guys

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:18.760
to use to inspire you to clone
it, to find some, I don't know,

00:31:18.760 --> 00:31:23.090
some queries in there and
open up some requests for us.

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:25.190
This has been running
for a few days already.

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:28.100
And the way, this is running,
I wanted to show you,

00:31:28.100 --> 00:31:29.879
this is running on--
this has been set up

00:31:29.879 --> 00:31:31.045
using the Terraform scripts.

00:31:31.045 --> 00:31:34.130
So if you guys go
to our repo here,

00:31:34.130 --> 00:31:35.870
it will take you to GitLab.

00:31:35.870 --> 00:31:38.550
And you have all the code here.

00:31:38.550 --> 00:31:40.742
So you can go into
the Drupal, WordPress,

00:31:40.742 --> 00:31:42.200
and inside Drupal
or WordPress, you

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:44.060
will see all the code
that we literally

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:46.496
used to build this site.

00:31:46.496 --> 00:31:47.620
And how does the site look?

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:48.900
Well this is Google Platform.

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:52.160
We have a project called,
very aptly, cloudlamp.org.

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:53.720
And we can see here
everything that

00:31:53.720 --> 00:31:56.510
has been happening on our
site for the last few weeks.

00:31:56.510 --> 00:31:58.814
Actually, we can see everything
that is happening now.

00:31:58.814 --> 00:32:00.230
We have an audit
log of everything

00:32:00.230 --> 00:32:03.300
that people are doing from
[INAUDIBLE] standpoint

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:06.090
and also that the
automation is doing itself.

00:32:06.090 --> 00:32:07.190
So how is this running?

00:32:07.190 --> 00:32:08.850
This is running the
Kubernetes cluster.

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:12.350
In this case, the cluster
starts with three nodes.

00:32:12.350 --> 00:32:13.850
And we have a node
pool that allows

00:32:13.850 --> 00:32:17.427
us to get scale from 3
to 10, depending on load.

00:32:17.427 --> 00:32:19.010
So if you guys hit
it at the same time

00:32:19.010 --> 00:32:21.044
and launch a denial
of service against us,

00:32:21.044 --> 00:32:22.710
probably these things
should be scaling.

00:32:22.710 --> 00:32:23.585
Please don't do that.

00:32:23.585 --> 00:32:26.880
The demo gods would
get really angry.

00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:29.580
But we have some storage
associated with it.

00:32:29.580 --> 00:32:32.900
And as you guys can see, we're
creating a volume based on NFS

00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:34.880
and attaching that
to the containers.

00:32:34.880 --> 00:32:38.810
We can see also the
YAML at some point.

00:32:38.810 --> 00:32:40.850
And then on the
workloads, specifically we

00:32:40.850 --> 00:32:43.430
are running the workload
that runs the site itself,

00:32:43.430 --> 00:32:46.490
the PHP and Apache piece is
a replication controller.

00:32:46.490 --> 00:32:48.972
Somebody may be asking, why
did you guys use a deployment.

00:32:48.972 --> 00:32:51.180
It turns out that we want
to automate the whole thing

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:51.680
end-to-end.

00:32:51.680 --> 00:32:53.330
We used Terraform for that.

00:32:53.330 --> 00:32:56.060
And you may know that Terraform
is an open source project that

00:32:56.060 --> 00:32:57.350
runs with providers.

00:32:57.350 --> 00:33:01.010
So the Kubernetes provider that
was available for Terraform

00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:03.590
when we set this up didn't
support deployments yet.

00:33:03.590 --> 00:33:05.845
So we decided to use a
replication controller.

00:33:05.845 --> 00:33:07.970
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: Hold
that thought just a second.

00:33:07.970 --> 00:33:09.110
What you're looking
at right here

00:33:09.110 --> 00:33:10.730
is actually the
Kubernetes dashboard

00:33:10.730 --> 00:33:12.182
that's built into GCP.

00:33:12.182 --> 00:33:13.640
So if you're
wondering to yourself,

00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:16.070
well, Kubernetes is fun to
get running and whatnot,

00:33:16.070 --> 00:33:18.080
but then I have to run
that wonderful web UI.

00:33:18.080 --> 00:33:19.490
And how do I run that, and
what does it look like?

00:33:19.490 --> 00:33:21.400
This is actually built
into the console.

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:23.137
This will show you
not only the services

00:33:23.137 --> 00:33:25.220
that you have deployed in
this particular cluster,

00:33:25.220 --> 00:33:27.620
but any clusters that this
project has access to,

00:33:27.620 --> 00:33:28.860
which is pretty darn cool.

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:31.068
So you can see the memory
that we're currently using,

00:33:31.068 --> 00:33:33.320
the CPU, disk, and a bunch
of other different metrics,

00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:35.069
and alerts, and
things like that.

00:33:35.069 --> 00:33:36.110
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: Mm-hmm.

00:33:36.110 --> 00:33:37.970
What we can also see is
that we have pods here.

00:33:37.970 --> 00:33:39.680
We have the different
pods with the containers

00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:40.370
that are running our things.

00:33:40.370 --> 00:33:42.650
So we have three containers
sharing that state.

00:33:42.650 --> 00:33:45.410
And each one of those
pods has two things.

00:33:45.410 --> 00:33:47.580
It has the Drupal pod.

00:33:47.580 --> 00:33:49.580
And the Drupal pod, we
can actually look into it

00:33:49.580 --> 00:33:51.560
and see how the actual
Drupal pod is getting

00:33:51.560 --> 00:33:52.700
requests and getting stuff.

00:33:52.700 --> 00:33:55.054
It looks like people are
hitting it, which is cool.

00:33:55.054 --> 00:33:57.470
Then we have another pod, which
is called Cloud SQL Proxy.

00:33:57.470 --> 00:33:59.600
What Cloud SQL Proxy
does is actually

00:33:59.600 --> 00:34:02.370
adds a secure proxy between
each one of the containers

00:34:02.370 --> 00:34:03.207
and the database.

00:34:03.207 --> 00:34:04.790
Everything is encrypted
in the middle.

00:34:04.790 --> 00:34:06.560
The database is
completely secured.

00:34:06.560 --> 00:34:09.080
So the connection between the
containers and the database

00:34:09.080 --> 00:34:11.790
is fully encrypted and proxied
through this container.

00:34:11.790 --> 00:34:14.531
So what we do is we use as a
sidecar to the main container.

00:34:14.531 --> 00:34:16.530
So the Drupal container
thinks that the database

00:34:16.530 --> 00:34:20.330
is a local host, is
in 127.0.0.1:3306.

00:34:20.330 --> 00:34:23.300
The proxy is getting that
securely, transporting that

00:34:23.300 --> 00:34:24.384
to the Cloud SQL database.

00:34:24.384 --> 00:34:26.383
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: What's
interesting about that

00:34:26.383 --> 00:34:29.360
is Cloud SQL, being a managed
SQL instance and a managed SQL

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:31.697
service for anyone
to take advantage of,

00:34:31.697 --> 00:34:33.780
we don't want just sort
of standard authentication

00:34:33.780 --> 00:34:36.114
that people can take advantage
of or exploit or whatnot.

00:34:36.114 --> 00:34:37.613
If you remember
back in those slides

00:34:37.613 --> 00:34:39.072
that we had about
managed security,

00:34:39.072 --> 00:34:40.821
this was one of the
features that we want.

00:34:40.821 --> 00:34:42.659
So we want better
authentication in order

00:34:42.659 --> 00:34:45.420
to be able to establish a
connection to the backend.

00:34:45.420 --> 00:34:48.300
And this allows you to do that
without your developers having

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:50.310
to understand any of
that sort of thing.

00:34:50.310 --> 00:34:51.780
Like Fernando
mentioned, they just

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:55.570
think that it's local host
running on what, 3306?

00:34:55.570 --> 00:34:56.850
And then they're all set.

00:34:56.850 --> 00:34:59.040
Deploying this in
a sidecar fashion

00:34:59.040 --> 00:35:01.470
ends up improving your
ability to actually leverage

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:04.440
the platform, manage a lot
of the complexity that's

00:35:04.440 --> 00:35:09.000
involved with it, but with still
getting the advantages of being

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:10.740
able to not have
things just exposed

00:35:10.740 --> 00:35:12.640
to the rest of the
internet and whatnot.

00:35:12.640 --> 00:35:12.990
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: Correct.

00:35:12.990 --> 00:35:15.080
So what else do we have on the
services side of the Kubernetes

00:35:15.080 --> 00:35:15.579
dashboard?

00:35:15.579 --> 00:35:18.495
We can see basically the load
balancing/service discovery

00:35:18.495 --> 00:35:19.780
piece of it.

00:35:19.780 --> 00:35:23.160
We have basically
two things here.

00:35:23.160 --> 00:35:26.530
We certainly have a service for
those that are Kubernetes fans.

00:35:26.530 --> 00:35:28.530
This is a node port
service, because that's what

00:35:28.530 --> 00:35:30.550
we used to connect the ingress.

00:35:30.550 --> 00:35:32.100
And we also created
an ingress, which

00:35:32.100 --> 00:35:36.000
instantiates a load balancer,
an HTTPS to TPS load balancer.

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:39.120
In this case, we created a TLS
certificate with Let's Encrypt.

00:35:39.120 --> 00:35:41.412
And we uploaded that to the
Google Cloud Load Balancer.

00:35:41.412 --> 00:35:42.786
So the Google
Cloud Load Balancer

00:35:42.786 --> 00:35:44.800
controlled by that ingress
is doing all the TLS

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:48.450
processing for us, and
it's completely off loaded

00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:51.837
from all our containers.

00:35:51.837 --> 00:35:52.920
What else do we have here?

00:35:52.920 --> 00:35:55.440
Obviously, this
eventually runs on VMs.

00:35:55.440 --> 00:35:58.150
In this case, we decided
to initially use--

00:35:58.150 --> 00:36:01.260
well these are the three
instances or our Kubernetes

00:36:01.260 --> 00:36:02.140
cluster.

00:36:02.140 --> 00:36:04.470
You may notice that we
don't see the masters.

00:36:04.470 --> 00:36:06.730
That's because the control
plane is handled by Google.

00:36:06.730 --> 00:36:08.430
We don't need to care
about the masters.

00:36:08.430 --> 00:36:10.846
We don't need to care about
making them bigger or smaller.

00:36:10.846 --> 00:36:12.930
Google will do that for us.

00:36:12.930 --> 00:36:14.880
They'll make sure that
the masters are up

00:36:14.880 --> 00:36:15.755
and they're working.

00:36:15.755 --> 00:36:17.880
The other thing that we
have here is an NFS server.

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:19.505
We went with a pooled
managed solution,

00:36:19.505 --> 00:36:21.724
because Filestore was
not really as well known

00:36:21.724 --> 00:36:22.890
when we wrote this software.

00:36:22.890 --> 00:36:24.810
But now you can
use Cloud Filestore

00:36:24.810 --> 00:36:26.797
and just create a
managed version of that.

00:36:26.797 --> 00:36:28.380
The code that you
will see in the repo

00:36:28.380 --> 00:36:30.750
has an NFS server, which
is just a simple VM.

00:36:30.750 --> 00:36:31.830
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: And we're
working with the open source

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:33.880
community, particularly
people that

00:36:33.880 --> 00:36:36.840
are working on Terraform to
make sure that that gets updated

00:36:36.840 --> 00:36:39.390
so you can actually describe
your Filestore instance

00:36:39.390 --> 00:36:41.610
inside of Terraform and
then get all of this stuff

00:36:41.610 --> 00:36:43.030
automated in the future.

00:36:43.030 --> 00:36:45.600
What's important to note though
is that backend, whatever

00:36:45.600 --> 00:36:48.241
it happens to be, as long as
it's read many, write many,

00:36:48.241 --> 00:36:49.740
it doesn't really
matter what it is.

00:36:49.740 --> 00:36:50.430
It's modular.

00:36:50.430 --> 00:36:51.630
It's kind of plug-and-play.

00:36:51.630 --> 00:36:53.020
And Kubernetes doesn't care.

00:36:53.020 --> 00:36:56.220
You give it a persistent volume
claim and a persistent volume.

00:36:56.220 --> 00:36:59.520
And you just say, make sure
that those bits on those disks

00:36:59.520 --> 00:37:01.230
are mounted in those containers.

00:37:01.230 --> 00:37:02.400
And then you're all set.

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:03.851
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: Yeah but
again, the general philosophy

00:37:03.851 --> 00:37:06.354
is I don't want to be
caring about this server.

00:37:06.354 --> 00:37:08.520
If somebody does it for me,
keeps it up and running,

00:37:08.520 --> 00:37:10.320
scales it up and down
for me, and I just

00:37:10.320 --> 00:37:12.840
pay for what I use, it's much
better than me making sure

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:15.270
that that NFS server is
up, that it's being patched

00:37:15.270 --> 00:37:17.040
with the latest and
greatest security,

00:37:17.040 --> 00:37:19.140
that it won't reboot on
a Saturday at 3:00 AM

00:37:19.140 --> 00:37:20.550
or something like that, right?

00:37:20.550 --> 00:37:21.720
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: We
can save $13 a month

00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:23.140
if we resize it, though.

00:37:23.140 --> 00:37:24.390
FERNANDO SANCHEZ: That's cool.

00:37:24.390 --> 00:37:25.950
We could save some
money to Google.

00:37:25.950 --> 00:37:28.580
We have some external
IP addresses.

00:37:28.580 --> 00:37:31.110
The other thing that we did is
creating a static IP address

00:37:31.110 --> 00:37:33.401
and linking that static IP
address to the load balancer

00:37:33.401 --> 00:37:35.730
so that we can
point it to the DNS.

00:37:35.730 --> 00:37:37.920
And the other thing
that we did is also

00:37:37.920 --> 00:37:40.830
controlling the entire DNS
configuration of the site

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:42.114
from Google Cloud DNS.

00:37:42.114 --> 00:37:43.530
So we can configure
it from there.

00:37:43.530 --> 00:37:45.490
And we can eventually
agreed subzones.

00:37:45.490 --> 00:37:47.550
And at some point, we
can put other sections.

00:37:47.550 --> 00:37:50.400
We may create an area for
import, another for export,

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:53.130
and another for
architecture at some point.

00:37:53.130 --> 00:37:55.130
The last piece that we
have here is Stackdriver.

00:37:55.130 --> 00:37:57.480
And Stackdriver does all
the monitoring for us.

00:37:57.480 --> 00:37:59.400
We didn't do a lot of
work on Stackdriver.

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:01.500
I'm sure you guys can
find Stackdriver guys here

00:38:01.500 --> 00:38:03.480
with much better
dashboards than ours.

00:38:03.480 --> 00:38:05.680
But what I have is a
simple uptime check.

00:38:05.680 --> 00:38:08.274
So in my welcome to Cloud LAMP--

00:38:08.274 --> 00:38:10.940
actually, this is
an old file now.

00:38:10.940 --> 00:38:15.650
Anyway, we have some monitors
and apparently my internet

00:38:15.650 --> 00:38:16.770
connection is failing.

00:38:16.770 --> 00:38:17.980
But that was pretty much it.

00:38:17.980 --> 00:38:22.170
So please, check the site.

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:23.640
Please, check the repo.

00:38:23.640 --> 00:38:26.070
And, obviously,
the idea with this

00:38:26.070 --> 00:38:29.222
is for us to build this
and learn as we go.

00:38:29.222 --> 00:38:31.430
So we will basically upload
in-- we will be basically

00:38:31.430 --> 00:38:33.013
